---
/**
 * The hero — one viewport tall, with everything inside it.
 * See docs/hero.md.
 *
 * Vertical structure is a two-row grid, not a flex column with justify-end:
 * flex pushed the whole block to the bottom and left a dead field above the
 * headline. The rows are copy / stage, and only the stage row flexes, so the
 * copy keeps its position near the top at any viewport height. There used to be
 * a third row above them for the nav; the nav is fixed over the whole document
 * now (SiteNav.astro) and the box pays for it in padding instead.
 *
 * The stage row is `minmax(0, 1fr)` and the frame inside it is `h-full`, so the
 * stage takes exactly the height that is left over and never a pixel more. The
 * hero used to crop the stage against the section's bottom edge as a scroll
 * cue; the maintainer retired that on 2026-08-29 — the whole hero has to be
 * readable in one viewport, with clear air under the frame.
 *
 * Spacing above the copy is in `svh`, so the proportions hold on a short laptop
 * screen and a tall monitor alike.
 */
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import Container from "@/components/Container.astro";
import DemoStage from "@/components/demo/DemoStage";
import SectionTrack from "@/components/SectionTrack.astro";
import { DOCS_URL, INSTALL_ID, TOP_ID } from "@/lib/nav";
import backdrop from "@/assets/hero/stage-backdrop.webp";

// The frame is the `content` step, so on a wide screen the backdrop only
// ever needs that column's width. Kept out of the markup because the style
// gate reads `width:` in a media condition as a content width.
const backdropSizes = "(min-width: 1400px) 1280px, 100vw"; // layout-allow: an <img sizes> fetch hint, not a layout width
---

<!--
  ONE VIEWPORT MINUS A TAIL, NOT EXACTLY ONE.

  The hero closes on its own stage frame — the box under the headline draws a
  bottom rule like every other section's band does. At exactly `100svh` that
  line lands on the fold's own pixel row and the window edge eats it, so the
  first screen opened with a rule under the nav and had nothing to answer it at
  the bottom: the page just ran out from under the reader.

  Holding the section one `--section-inset` short pulls the line up into the
  screen with air beneath it — and it is `--section-inset` rather than the
  `--space-xxl` it used to be so that the tail matches what every section below
  spends on the same job. The joint with the logo strip is then the standard
  one: this section's closing line, `2 x --section-inset` of air, that band's
  opening line. See docs/layout.md § "One boundary, one shape".

  THE HERO HAS NO EDGES OF ITS OWN, and that is the point of this screen. There
  is no rule across the top and no rails down the sides: the page chrome is held
  at nothing while the hero owns the screen and arrives as the reader scrolls
  out of it — see layout.css § "The page chrome arrives". A framed field is a
  form; the first screen is meant to read as open ground.
-->
<section
  id={TOP_ID}
  class="relative grid min-h-[calc(100svh_-_var(--section-inset))] grid-rows-[minmax(0,1fr)] overflow-hidden"
>
  <!--
    THE HERO'S OWN BOX. It stays a separate element from the `<section>` — the
    section is what the reader scrolls past, this is what they look at — and it
    is what `SectionTrack` measures, since the track measures the box it is
    dropped into.

    IT PAYS FOR THE NAV ITSELF, in padding. Until 2026-08-29 the nav was a row
    of this section's own grid, so the hero's content began under it for free.
    The nav is fixed over the whole document now (SiteNav.astro) and is out of
    the flow entirely, so without this the headline would start behind it. The
    figure is the same `--nav-height` the row used to be, which is what keeps
    every proportion in docs/hero.md exactly where it was.

    `open`, because NOTHING RAIL-TO-RAIL IS DRAWN AT THIS BOX'S BOTTOM EDGE.
    What closes the hero is its own stage frame, and that is a rounded card
    75px higher up — the section spends its last `--section-inset` on the tail
    that pulls the card's line clear of the fold. Closed, the square turned off
    the rail at the bottom of this box and ran across bare ground to the other
    one; the maintainer's report on 2026-08-29 was that it "is not quite on the
    line". Open, it comes down the rail and carries straight on into the logo
    strip's opening rule, which is the next line that actually exists.
  -->
  <div
    class="relative grid min-h-0 grid-rows-[auto_minmax(0,1fr)] pt-[var(--nav-height)]"
  >
    <SectionTrack open />

    <div class="pt-[5svh]">
      <Container width="prose">
        <!--
          Capped well below the old 6rem: the headline is the one element that
          decides whether the stage still has room, and three lines of it is the
          shape the maintainer asked for (2026-08-29).
        -->
        <h1
          class="text-center text-balance leading-[1.08] tracking-[-1.5px]"
          style="font-size: clamp(1.875rem, 1.3rem + 1.5vw, 3.25rem)"
        >
          Speak to your computer and it does the work
        </h1>

        <div class="mt-7 flex flex-col justify-center gap-3 sm:flex-row">
          <a
            href={`#${INSTALL_ID}`}
            class="inline-flex min-h-[44px] items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-ink px-6 text-[15px] font-medium text-on-ink transition-colors hover:bg-ink-active"
          >
            Download for Windows
          </a>
          {/*
            This used to point at `#docs`, an id no element on this page has
            ever carried — the button scrolled nowhere. The docs live in the
            repository until this site grows a route of its own; the URL is a
            constant in src/lib/nav.ts so there is one place to change it then.
          */}
          <a
            href={DOCS_URL}
            class="inline-flex min-h-[44px] items-center justify-center rounded-full border border-hairline-strong bg-card px-6 text-[15px] font-medium text-ink transition-colors hover:border-ink"
          >
            Read the docs
          </a>
        </div>
      </Container>
    </div>

    <!--
      The stage row. `min-h-0` lets the row shrink past its content's intrinsic
      height — without it the grid track grows to fit the board and the hero
      spills past the fold again.

      The padding is 6svh and not the 10svh it was, because the band under the
      frame is now built from two pieces rather than one: this padding, then the
      section's 48px tail below the closing rule. Together they come to the same
      distance the old single padding did, so the frame keeps the height it had
      — the band beneath it simply gained a line partway down.
    -->
    <Container width="content" class="min-h-0 pt-6 pb-[6svh]">
      <!--
        `hairline-strong`, not the usual hairline (kept from 09943cb): the frame
        now holds a pale painting on a near-black page, and the stronger edge is
        what stops that bright rectangle from bleeding into the floor around it.
        The reason has changed with the backdrop; the choice has not.
      -->
      <div
        class="hero-frame relative h-full min-h-[300px] overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-hairline-strong"
      >
        <!--
          The backdrop. An original painting generated for this site, so there is
          no third-party licence attached to it — see docs/hero.md, "Backdrop".
          Eager and above the fold; `object-cover` because the frame's own ratio
          moves with the viewport height.
        -->
        <Image
          src={backdrop}
          alt=""
          widths={[1280, 1920, 2560]}
          sizes={backdropSizes}
          loading="eager"
          class="pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 h-full w-full object-cover"
        />
        <DemoStage client:load className="relative h-full" />
      </div>
    </Container>
  </div>
</section>

<style is:global>
  /*
   * `<astro-island>` is an unknown element, so it computes to `display: inline`
   * and carries no height of its own. The stage inside it asks for
   * `height: 100%`, which against an inline parent resolves to auto — the stage
   * then falls back to its own aspect ratio and stops fitting the frame. On a
   * short viewport that puts the bottom of the window outside the frame, which
   * is the one thing this hero is built to prevent.
   *
   * `is:global` because the wrapper is generated by the hydration runtime and
   * never carries this component's scope attribute.
   */
  .hero-frame > astro-island {
    display: block;
    height: 100%;
  }
</style>
